WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.570
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Did you
know that 87% of data science

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.120
projects never make
it into production?

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.440
That's just one out of
every 10 AI projects.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.610
So how do you make sure
your project does not

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.340
become one of those 87%?

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.780
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this video,

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:23.310
I will walk you through steps
to operationalize your machine

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:26.340
learning workflow
using vertex pipelines,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.760
so your project becomes one
of those 10 successful AI

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.820
projects.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.650
We will look at MLOps framework
and vertex AI pipelines, which

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.470
helps you break down the
complex multi-step ML

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.460
workflow into a pipeline and
ensure that each step runs

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.910
in a reproducible, auditable,
cost-effective, and a scalable

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:45.540
way.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.090
In contrast with typical
software systems, which

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:51.000
are traditionally
code-centric, ML systems

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.870
involve an intricate
relationship between data,

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.240
code, and the models.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.000
Now, data and models
are unique artifacts

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.620
with their own
dependencies and pitfalls.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.190
And the joint management
of these three artifacts

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.590
is a challenge in
delivering and maintaining

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.840
production machine
learning systems.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.140
That is what MLOps is.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.900
MLOps is a culture
and practice that

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.250
aims to unify ML system
development and operations

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.620
and guide through the
challenges of taking

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:26.130
ML projects from
experimentation to production.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.860
It is just like
DevOps, but for ML.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.370
Hence, MLOps, which handles
managing the life cycle

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.430
of data, models, and code.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.500
Let's start with a
high-level MLOps framework.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.520
So the first step
is ML development,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.830
which is inherently
experimental, where

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.750
data scientists
and model builders

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.120
explore and transform
the data sets,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.300
explore different algorithms,
train many models,

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.560
and then compare them.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.860
Then the second step
is continuous training.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.310
Now, production training should
be automatable and repeatable

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:02.430
so it can take new
data or other triggers

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:06.060
and generate new and
better models on demand.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.210
And then the third step
is model deployment,

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.670
which is really all about
continuous integration

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:12.990
and continuous delivery.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:16.470
This involves running
A/B tests, evaluation

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:20.070
of model behavior in production,
and then approving them

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:24.270
for releasing, following a
rigorous, auditable, and even

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.490
reversible CIDC process.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.760
And then lastly, it's
continuous monitoring.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.040
You need to continuously
monitor your models

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.890
when they are running in
production, because you

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.620
want to get a sense of
how they are performing.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.770
This is important to ensure
the quality and business

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:44.820
continuity, but also crucial
for getting high-quality signals

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:48.600
into how to improve a model
for your next iteration.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:53.100
Model management and
governance is basically

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.790
the step that applies
to all of these steps.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.160
It's the entire framework.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:03.510
We need traceability,
verifiability, and auditability

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.470
at each stage of this workflow.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:09.810
ML pipeline models
this MLOps workflow.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.720
A pipeline is a way
of modeling a workflow

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.730
as a set of connected steps.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:19.410
Each step takes as input the
output of the previous step,

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.020
and performs some
additional computations,

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.780
and then produces outputs
that can be utilized

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.470
by the future components.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.650
Now, here in this
MLOps workflow,

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.780
to keep the spirit
of experimentation

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.750
in the ML development
phase, we recommend

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.410
using the reusable
training pipelines.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.930
And the reason for
reusable pipelines

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:43.590
is because the data scientists
can share components

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.000
amongst themselves,
so they don't

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.110
have to start with
a blinking cursor

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:51.630
and to rerun and iterate quickly
to create those optimal models.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:56.550
The output of this step would
be the trained pipeline source

00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:00.640
code, which you feed into the
continuous training phase.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.930
The pipeline
generated earlier can

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.300
be now treated like a
software application

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:10.470
and is version-controlled and
deployed through CIDC process

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.720
as a training
pipeline that can now

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.450
be invoked with new
data or parameters

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.350
in production as needed.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:21.870
Now, the output of this step
would be your trained model.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.990
And then the output of
the model deployment

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.220
is your live predictions.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:30.900
And these predictions
can not just

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:35.100
be predictions, but also
logs and other records

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.350
of production inferences.

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.900
Now, the output of
continuous monitoring--

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.130
because you are monitoring
the model continuously,

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.170
the output of this
continuous monitoring

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:49.690
is those alerts that
go out to the team,

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:53.850
so that they can continuously
measure and monitor

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:57.540
what the model's performance is
looking like-- is it degrading

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.240
or triggering
rollbacks-- and retaining

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.690
A/B testing of any
candidate models.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:06.600
The model management and
governance capabilities,

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.640
obviously, as I
said earlier, span

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.340
across the entire
framework, including

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.450
the features such as, model
registry, model approvals,

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.990
and model provenance.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:20.060
Now, what are vertex pipelines?

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.330
Vertex pipelines help you
automate and monitor your ML

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:27.260
systems by orchestrating
this MLOps workflow

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.120
in a serverless manner.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.090
They are based on
containers, which

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:38.090
help you make the MLOps process
really portable and scalable.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:41.630
And the vertex pipelines
store the workflow artifacts

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:46.280
using vertex AI ML metadata,
which makes it easier

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.820
to analyze the lineage
of our workflow

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:52.520
and the items that
are being created.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:55.100
For example, an
ML model's lineage

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:59.450
may include training data, the
hyper-parameters, and the code

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:02.000
that we use to create
the models, which

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.520
are critical pieces of
information to understand

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.510
the changes in performance or
accuracy of our ML systems.

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:12.740
Now, as you can imagine managing
this metadata by yourself

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:16.250
in an ad hoc manner can be
difficult and time-consuming,

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:18.890
and vertex pipelines
makes it easy to do that.

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:23.390
Now, vertex pipelines support
both Kubeflow pipelines

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:27.020
and also TensorFlow Extended.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:30.110
Now, if you are already
using TensorFlow,

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:32.700
TensorFlow Extended is
a good choice for you.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.820
It provides a rich
set of components

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:36.950
that helps you take
the TensorFlow code

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:39.380
and make it into an ML pipeline.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:41.880
I did a video on TFX
not too long ago,

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:44.400
which I will include in
the description below.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.520
Now, if you're not
using TensorFlow,

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:51.380
then use KFP, which is an
open-source machine learning

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:52.040
pipeline.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:54.800
It offers a great
deal of flexibility.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.430
It's easy to plug in code
from any ML framework,

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:01.460
including the normal frameworks
that aren't Python-based,

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:03.320
such as Apache MXNet.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:07.190
Now, you might ask, why
do I need vertex pipelines

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.950
if I use Kubeflow
pipelines already today?

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:14.060
Now, the biggest reason
is that it is managed.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.640
And because it is
managed, you don't

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:21.200
have to maintain or create
servers by yourself.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:23.360
Now, when you're
using KFP, you need

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.270
to build, scale, and
maintain a Kubernetes cluster

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:27.560
all by yourself.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.840
But with managed
pipelines, we don't

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.490
have to do any of that work.

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:32.930
It's all serverless.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:36.140
OK, so in this video,
we got a quick overview

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:39.500
of MLOps workflow
and vertex pipelines.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:40.310
What's next?

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:43.040
Let's take a look at
vertex pipelines demo

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:44.670
in our next video.

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.450
In the meantime, try out
the codelab linked below,

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:51.370
and subscribe to get notified
about the next video.

